The recall search returned **no record listing the fictional lot `DEMO-LOT-404`**. That means this query did not find an exact lot match; it does **not** establish that the product is safe, unaffected, or absent from other evidence. [S1](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22diphenhydramine%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20)

Relevant children’s diphenhydramine recall records included:

- **CORRECTDOSE Children’s Allergy Relief**, diphenhydramine oral solution: lot **21011A**, expired January 2023; Class II; status **Terminated**. [S18](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1492-2022%22)
- **CVS Health Children’s Allergy Relief Liquid Medication**, diphenhydramine oral solution: lot **80060**, expired November 2020; Class II; status **Terminated**. [S19](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1631-2019%22)
- **DrKids Children’s Allergy Relief**, diphenhydramine oral solution: lot **90003**, expired January 2021; Class II; status **Terminated**. [S20](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1632-2019%22)
- A Family Dollar recall listed products including **Benadryl D Children’s Allergy Sinus** and **Good Sense Child Allergy**, but identified products by store SKU rather than individual lot; status **Ongoing**. [S15](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1450-2022%22)

### What a missing lot match means

It may mean the lot is not listed in the records returned by this search, or that the notice uses a different identifier—such as a lot range, “all lots,” distribution dates, SKU, UPC, or NDC. It may also reflect differences in product formulation, manufacturer, brand, or packaging. This lookup cannot determine safety or clear the bottle.

### Details to check

Compare the package with the official notice or have a pharmacist or manufacturer verify:

- exact product and formulation;
- brand and label name;
- manufacturer and distributor;
- strength and package size;
- NDC, UPC, or SKU;
- complete lot number, including letters, zeros, hyphens, and spacing;
- expiration or beyond-use date;
- whether the notice covers a lot range, all lots, distribution dates, or selected retailers;
- recall status and official consumer instructions.

The separate FDA safety-communications search returned a 2020 notice about serious problems associated with high doses of diphenhydramine; it was not a lot-specific recall. [S24](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-warns-about-serious-problems-high-doses-allergy-medicine-diphenhydramine-benadryl)

Informational only; not medical advice. Compiled from public NIH/NLM/FDA data, which may be incomplete or out of date. Patients should talk to a clinician, pharmacist or poison control before starting, stopping or changing any medication.

